signature.advancedArgs:
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "employeeNumber": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The team member's employee number, used ONLY when the user volunteers a number themselves. Never ask the user for an employee number, employee ID or staff number, and never look one up with another tool: a name alone is enough, because this tool resolves it against the supervisor's own subordinate list, which already carries every number. If the user gives a name, send employeeName and leave this out. Call with both omitted to get the subordinate list rather than guessing. Anyone who is not a direct subordinate is refused and the team list returned. For the signed-in employee's own benefit application, use selfEmployeeBenefitApllication instead."
    },
    "employeeName": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The team member's name as the user said it - a first name alone (e.g. 'Liam') is fine and is the normal case. As soon as the user names someone, call this tool with that name: do not ask for their employee number, and do not ask any other question first. Never resolve the name with getGlobalEmployeeSearch or any other employee-lookup tool, because those search the whole company while this application may only ever be for one of this supervisor's own direct subordinates. This tool matches the name against that subordinate list itself and, once matched, has everything it needs to identify the employee. If several subordinates match, it returns just those candidates - show the user only those and ask which one, never a wider list of people with that name."
    },
    "benefitType": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The benefit type to apply for, named as the user says it (e.g. 'Medical Claims'). Never ask the user to name a benefit type before you have called this tool, and NEVER offer types from your own knowledge (Health Insurance, Life Insurance, Cash Benefit and the like are not real options here) - the types differ per employee and per company. When the user has not named one, call this tool with benefitType omitted and it returns that employee's own live availableBenefitTypes; show the user exactly that list and ask which one. If the name they give is not on it, or matches more than one, the tool returns the candidates to choose from. Never ask for a code."
    },
    "fieldValues": {
      "type": "object",
      "description": "For benefit types built from plain fields: the values, keyed by each field's exact displayName from this tool's own discovery response. Call with benefitType set and fieldValues omitted first to get that list with each field's mandatory flag, input type and options, then ask the user only for those fields. Never ask for fields you have imagined (benefit plan, policy number and so on): they differ per benefit type and per employee, and only the discovery response is real. The application date is always required even when it says not-mandatory, because entitlement, utilized amount and the total are all calculated for that date - ask which date rather than assuming today. Entitlement figures belong to the date they were fetched for and to no other: to answer 'what is the entitlement on 2 September', or whenever the user names or changes the date, call this tool again with fieldValues containing just that application date and report the figures it returns. Never carry an entitlement figure across from an earlier date, and never state one without a tool call behind it. Use a dropdown option's text, never a code; dates in the format the discovery response states; amounts as plain numbers; yes/no fields as 'yes' or 'no'. 'Total Request' is the amount Benefit History records; PeoplesHR calculates it from the amount field(s), so leave it out unless the supervisor explicitly wants a different total. Entitlement, Utilized Amount and Balance are never inputs. The Total Request field is one of these: it is editable on the screen, so when the supervisor asks for a particular Total Request, put that figure here under the Total Request field name and leave the amount field(s) exactly as they gave them. It does not have to match the amount, and it must never be refused as \"calculated\" unless this tool itself says PeoplesHR locks it.",
      "additionalProperties": {
        "type": "string"
      }
    },
    "rows": {
      "type": "array",
      "description": "For benefit types claimed as rows (the discovery response sets rowsRequired and lists gridColumns instead of fields): one object per claim row, each keyed by the exact column displayName from that response. Ask the user for one row per claim - e.g. one per bill - and never invent columns. Read-only columns (Department, Entitlement, Utilized Amount and the like) are filled in by PeoplesHR and must not be sent. Each row is validated by PeoplesHR as it is added, and a rejected row stops the application with the reason given.",
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "additionalProperties": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      }
    },
    "comment": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The applicant comment for this application. Only required when the discovery response's commentMandatory is true - check that rather than always asking. If showComment is false for the resolved benefit type, omit this entirely."
    },
    "confirmed": {
      "type": "boolean",
      "description": "Set true only after the supervisor has seen the preview this tool returns (the employee it is for, benefit type, every resolved value, entitlement/utilized/balance, the Total Request amount, any balanceWarning, the comment, and any confirmMessage from PeoplesHR) and has explicitly agreed to submit on that employee's behalf. Must be sent together with previewDigest. Omit or set false to preview without submitting - safe to call repeatedly while they are still deciding."
    },
    "previewDigest": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The previewDigest value from the preview response the supervisor actually saw, copied verbatim and sent alongside confirmed:true. It binds the confirmation to those exact figures: if the employee's entitlement or utilized amount has moved since, the tool returns a fresh preview instead of submitting. Never invent or reuse an older value - always copy the one from the most recent preview response."
    },
    "removeAttachments": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "type": "string"
      },
      "description": "File names to leave out of this application, exactly as shown in the preview's attachments list or as attached in the chat. Attachments are never passed as arguments: files the supervisor attaches in the chat are picked up automatically, checked against the benefit type's own allowed file types, maximum size and count, and uploaded when the preview is built. Several files can be attached - in one message or over several - and each is added alongside the ones already on the application; the preview lists them all, and every one still listed is submitted on confirmation. Use this when the supervisor asks to remove an attachment before confirming (it is deleted from the application), or to continue without a file the tool rejected. Keep passing the same list on every later call for this application, including the confirmation - otherwise a removed file still attached in the chat is uploaded again. Attachments cannot be removed after submission."
    },
    "attachmentSessionKey": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The attachmentSessionKey value from this tool's most recent response for this application, copied verbatim. Attachments are staged on PeoplesHR under this key before submission, so it must be sent on every later call for the same application - including the one with confirmed:true - or the staged files will not be found. Leave it out on the first call, and leave it out again if the employee or the benefit type changes."
    },
    "replaceAttachments": {
      "type": "boolean",
      "description": "Set true ONLY when the supervisor explicitly wants the file(s) attached in THIS message to REPLACE every document attached before them - \"change the document to this\", \"use this one instead\", \"replace the attachment\". The earlier files are then left out of the application (unless attached again), and the preview lists what remains. It is ignored when no usable file is attached in this message, so it can never empty the application. To swap just one file, put the old one in removeAttachments instead. Leave it out in every other case: every file attached in the conversation is part of the application, so a second or third file (\"add this attachment as well\", \"add this too\", \"here is another one\") is added alongside the others, and all of them are submitted on confirmation. Re-attaching a file under the same name replaces that one file by itself."
    },
    "totalRequest": {
      "type": "number",
      "description": "The Total Request figure, when the supervisor asks for a particular one (\"change the total request to 150\", \"keep the amount as 200 and the total as 150\"). The Benefit Application screen lets this figure be typed over: PeoplesHR fills it in from the amount field(s), but whatever it holds at submission is what Benefit History records, and it does NOT have to match the amount. Pass the figure here and leave the amount field(s) in fieldValues exactly as the supervisor gave them - never change an amount to match it, never offer to change the amount instead, and never answer that the two are linked or that the total cannot be changed. Leave this out unless a particular total was asked for. If PeoplesHR locks the figure for that benefit type, this tool says so itself."
    }
  },
  "required": []
}
